// EB Garamond posters: a 1592 title page, a Latin motto, the long s.
---
<figure class="pw-p" id="eb-garamond-title-page" data-name="Title page">
  <div class="pw-art eb-a">
    <p class="eb-a__t">THE<br /><span>ESSAYES</span></p>
    <p class="eb-a__s">or Morall, Politike and<br /><em>Millitarie Discourses</em></p>
    <span class="eb-a__orn" aria-hidden="true">❧</span>
    <p class="eb-a__by">Written in French by<br />Michaell de Montaigne</p>
    <span class="eb-a__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <p class="eb-a__imp">LONDON,<br /><em>Printed by Val. Sims for Edward Blount.</em><br />1603.</p>
  </div>
</figure>
<figure class="pw-p" id="eb-garamond-festina-lente" data-name="Festina lente">
  <div class="pw-art eb-b">
    <p class="eb-b__h">Festina<br /><em>lente</em></p>
    <p class="eb-b__t">Make haste, slowly.</p>
    <p class="eb-b__f">Motto of the Aldine Press, Venice, 1494</p>
  </div>
</figure>
<figure class="pw-p" id="eb-garamond-long-s" data-name="The long s">
  <div class="pw-art eb-c">
    <p class="eb-c__s">ſ</p>
    <p class="eb-c__t">Before 1800, an <em>s</em> in the middle of a word was written long: Congreſs, poſſeſſion, Miſſiſſippi.</p>
  </div>
</figure>
